The code statements that lead to the bug are:
dst = ip6_route_input_lookup(dev_net(skb->dev), skb->dev, &fl6, skb, flags);
if (dst->error)
    goto drop;

Reason:
The function `ip6_route_input_lookup()` returns a referenced `dst_entry` even when the route lookup yields an error (e.g., network unreachable). The subsequent `if (dst->error)` check jumps to the `drop` label without releasing the reference obtained from the lookup, thus leaking the `dst` entry on each such packet.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (dst->error) {
    dst_release(dst);
    goto drop;
}

Reason:
The fix adds `dst_release(dst)` inside the error check before jumping to `drop`. This ensures that the reference count on the `dst` entry is decremented, preventing memory leaks of `dst` entries when the route lookup returns an error.

